1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,710
On September 17,

2
00:00:02,110 --> 00:00:04,030
the Federal Reserve did something it

3
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:05,450
hadn't done in more than three

4
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:05,730
years.

5
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:07,510
It raised interest rates.

6
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:08,853
Then in October,

7
00:00:09,253 --> 00:00:10,153
it held them there,

8
00:00:10,413 --> 00:00:12,093
and two officials voted to raise

9
00:00:11,893 --> 00:00:12,593
them again.

10
00:00:13,062 --> 00:00:14,242
If you have a credit card,

11
00:00:14,482 --> 00:00:15,202
a car loan,

12
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:15,922
a mortgage,

13
00:00:16,082 --> 00:00:17,802
or even just a savings account,

14
00:00:17,902 --> 00:00:18,762
this affects you.

15
00:00:19,202 --> 00:00:20,382
Here's exactly how.

16
00:00:24,483 --> 00:00:24,963
First,

17
00:00:24,923 --> 00:00:26,583
what does the Fed actually control?

18
00:00:27,103 --> 00:00:28,323
It's not your mortgage rate,

19
00:00:28,463 --> 00:00:29,723
and it's not your credit card

20
00:00:29,523 --> 00:00:30,003
rate.

21
00:00:30,463 --> 00:00:30,883
At least,

22
00:00:31,023 --> 00:00:31,643
not directly.

23
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:33,667
It sets a target for the

24
00:00:33,467 --> 00:00:34,527
federal funds rate.

25
00:00:35,027 --> 00:00:36,687
That's the interest rate banks charge

26
00:00:36,487 --> 00:00:38,227
each other for overnight loans.

27
00:00:38,809 --> 00:00:39,429
Right now,

28
00:00:39,489 --> 00:00:43,009
that target is 3.75% to 4%.

29
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:44,833
When banks pay more to borrow,

30
00:00:45,213 --> 00:00:46,573
they charge you more to borrow.

31
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:48,993
That's how one number ripples through

32
00:00:48,793 --> 00:00:49,753
the whole economy.

33
00:00:50,275 --> 00:00:52,135
Congress gave the Fed two jobs:

34
00:00:52,635 --> 00:00:53,735
keep prices stable,

35
00:00:54,115 --> 00:00:55,695
and keep as many people working

36
00:00:55,495 --> 00:00:56,495
as possible.

37
00:00:57,095 --> 00:00:58,875
Economists call it the dual mandate,

38
00:00:59,235 --> 00:01:01,295
and those two jobs sometimes pull

39
00:01:01,095 --> 00:01:02,215
in opposite directions.

40
00:01:03,617 --> 00:01:05,237
Here's where that rate has been

41
00:01:05,037 --> 00:01:06,297
over the last ten years.

42
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:07,873
In early 2022,

43
00:01:07,773 --> 00:01:09,493
it was close to zero.

44
00:01:10,273 --> 00:01:11,533
Then inflation took off,

45
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:13,473
and the Fed raised rates 11

46
00:01:13,273 --> 00:01:15,193
times in about a year and

47
00:01:14,993 --> 00:01:15,513
a half,

48
00:01:15,753 --> 00:01:17,653
up to 5.5%.

49
00:01:18,219 --> 00:01:19,399
Once inflation cooled,

50
00:01:19,879 --> 00:01:20,719
it started cutting,

51
00:01:20,519 --> 00:01:23,059
in 2024 and 2025,

52
00:01:23,499 --> 00:01:26,159
down to a range of 3.5%

53
00:01:25,959 --> 00:01:28,619
to 3.75%.

54
00:01:28,945 --> 00:01:29,365
Then,

55
00:01:29,425 --> 00:01:31,005
in September 2026,

56
00:01:31,345 --> 00:01:33,165
it turned around and raised rates

57
00:01:32,965 --> 00:01:33,525
again.

58
00:01:34,717 --> 00:01:35,457
So why now?

59
00:01:35,597 --> 00:01:36,237
In one word:

60
00:01:36,257 --> 00:01:36,577
oil.

61
00:01:37,143 --> 00:01:38,803
After the war with Iran stopped

62
00:01:38,603 --> 00:01:40,463
tankers in the Strait of Hormuz,

63
00:01:40,743 --> 00:01:42,563
gas and diesel prices soared.

64
00:01:43,103 --> 00:01:44,383
We broke that down in our

65
00:01:44,183 --> 00:01:44,963
last video.

66
00:01:45,437 --> 00:01:48,657
Inflation climbed from about 2.4% in

67
00:01:48,457 --> 00:01:51,617
February to 3.7% in September,

68
00:01:52,197 --> 00:01:54,537
almost double the Fed's 2% target.

69
00:01:55,074 --> 00:01:56,594
The Fed's job is to keep

70
00:01:56,394 --> 00:01:57,374
prices stable,

71
00:01:57,834 --> 00:01:59,354
and its main tool is interest

72
00:01:59,154 --> 00:01:59,814
rates.

73
00:02:00,274 --> 00:02:02,574
Higher rates make borrowing more expensive,

74
00:02:03,034 --> 00:02:04,814
so people and businesses spend a

75
00:02:04,614 --> 00:02:05,234
little less,

76
00:02:05,454 --> 00:02:07,174
and that cools prices down.

77
00:02:07,719 --> 00:02:08,459
But there's a catch.

78
00:02:09,159 --> 00:02:10,779
The Fed can't pump more oil,

79
00:02:11,119 --> 00:02:12,919
and higher rates can't reopen a

80
00:02:12,719 --> 00:02:13,459
shipping lane.

81
00:02:13,839 --> 00:02:15,459
They can only slow spending,

82
00:02:15,679 --> 00:02:17,379
so a fuel shock doesn't turn

83
00:02:17,179 --> 00:02:18,859
into higher prices for everything.

84
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:21,090
You might wonder:

85
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:22,730
why doesn't the Fed just keep

86
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:24,450
rates low and make life easier?

87
00:02:24,938 --> 00:02:26,618
Because if inflation gets stuck,

88
00:02:26,978 --> 00:02:29,118
everyone's paycheck buys a little less

89
00:02:28,918 --> 00:02:30,098
every single year.

90
00:02:30,501 --> 00:02:32,841
That's what happened in the 1970s.

91
00:02:33,761 --> 00:02:36,001
Inflation hit about 15% in 1980,

92
00:02:36,641 --> 00:02:37,881
and the Fed had to push

93
00:02:37,681 --> 00:02:40,141
this rate to about 19% in

94
00:02:39,941 --> 00:02:41,681
1981 to break it,

95
00:02:41,841 --> 00:02:43,541
which helped cause a deep recession.

96
00:02:44,191 --> 00:02:44,691
Small,

97
00:02:44,691 --> 00:02:46,791
early steps today are meant to

98
00:02:46,591 --> 00:02:48,831
avoid ever needing medicine that strong

99
00:02:48,631 --> 00:02:49,151
again.

100
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:52,290
At its meeting on October 28,

101
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:53,910
the Fed held rates steady.

102
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:55,750
But the vote was 10 to

103
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:55,990
2,

104
00:02:56,110 --> 00:02:58,290
and both officials who disagreed wanted

105
00:02:58,090 --> 00:02:58,970
another hike.

106
00:02:59,454 --> 00:03:00,994
Twelve people vote on rates:

107
00:03:01,594 --> 00:03:03,594
the seven Fed governors in Washington,

108
00:03:03,974 --> 00:03:05,394
the president of the New York

109
00:03:05,194 --> 00:03:05,654
Fed,

110
00:03:05,814 --> 00:03:07,414
and four of the other regional

111
00:03:07,214 --> 00:03:08,234
Fed presidents,

112
00:03:08,034 --> 00:03:09,334
who take turns.

113
00:03:09,966 --> 00:03:11,706
That shows the debate inside the

114
00:03:11,506 --> 00:03:12,406
Fed right now.

115
00:03:12,966 --> 00:03:14,826
Some worry inflation is getting stuck.

116
00:03:15,326 --> 00:03:17,186
Others worry about slowing the economy

117
00:03:16,986 --> 00:03:17,826
too much.

118
00:03:18,302 --> 00:03:18,922
So far,

119
00:03:19,002 --> 00:03:20,662
the job market is holding up.

120
00:03:21,122 --> 00:03:23,882
Unemployment was 4.2% in September.

121
00:03:25,283 --> 00:03:26,583
Before we go through your bills,

122
00:03:26,883 --> 00:03:27,983
one important point.

123
00:03:28,523 --> 00:03:30,263
If your mortgage or car loan

124
00:03:30,063 --> 00:03:31,203
has a fixed rate,

125
00:03:31,403 --> 00:03:33,043
this hike doesn't change your payment

126
00:03:32,843 --> 00:03:33,463
at all.

127
00:03:33,918 --> 00:03:35,238
It matters for new loans,

128
00:03:35,038 --> 00:03:37,038
and for anything with a variable

129
00:03:36,838 --> 00:03:37,438
rate:

130
00:03:37,878 --> 00:03:38,598
credit cards,

131
00:03:38,838 --> 00:03:40,418
home equity lines of credit,

132
00:03:40,558 --> 00:03:41,858
and adjustable rate mortgages.

133
00:03:42,498 --> 00:03:43,878
Those can go up within a

134
00:03:43,678 --> 00:03:44,398
month or two.

135
00:03:45,617 --> 00:03:46,037
Now,

136
00:03:45,837 --> 00:03:47,077
what does this mean for you?

137
00:03:47,477 --> 00:03:49,057
Let's start with the most expensive

138
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:50,617
debt most people have:

139
00:03:51,017 --> 00:03:51,517
credit cards.

140
00:03:52,118 --> 00:03:54,298
Most credit cards have variable rates

141
00:03:54,098 --> 00:03:55,578
tied to the prime rate,

142
00:03:55,898 --> 00:03:57,678
which moves almost one for one

143
00:03:57,478 --> 00:03:58,178
with the Fed.

144
00:03:58,662 --> 00:04:00,782
The prime rate is now 7%,

145
00:04:00,582 --> 00:04:02,802
and the average interest rate on

146
00:04:02,602 --> 00:04:04,922
credit card accounts at banks is

147
00:04:04,722 --> 00:04:06,762
about 21.2%.

148
00:04:07,275 --> 00:04:09,555
If you carry a $5,000 balance

149
00:04:09,355 --> 00:04:10,175
at that rate,

150
00:04:10,495 --> 00:04:13,195
you'd pay about $1,060 a year

151
00:04:12,995 --> 00:04:13,655
in interest.

152
00:04:14,246 --> 00:04:16,306
This hike itself added a quarter

153
00:04:16,106 --> 00:04:17,426
of a percentage point.

154
00:04:17,946 --> 00:04:19,506
On that same $5,000,

155
00:04:20,086 --> 00:04:23,186
that's only about $12.50 more a

156
00:04:22,986 --> 00:04:23,406
year.

157
00:04:23,986 --> 00:04:26,246
The real problem is the 21%

158
00:04:26,046 --> 00:04:26,486
itself.

159
00:04:27,190 --> 00:04:28,950
That's why paying down card debt

160
00:04:28,750 --> 00:04:30,430
is one of the best guaranteed

161
00:04:30,230 --> 00:04:31,870
returns you can get.

162
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:34,150
Every dollar you pay off stops

163
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:36,530
costing you over 21% a year.

164
00:04:37,717 --> 00:04:39,697
Mortgage rates don't follow the Fed

165
00:04:39,497 --> 00:04:40,157
directly.

166
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:42,937
They follow what investors expect for

167
00:04:42,737 --> 00:04:44,917
inflation and interest rates over the

168
00:04:44,717 --> 00:04:45,817
next several years.

169
00:04:46,330 --> 00:04:47,190
In late February,

170
00:04:47,430 --> 00:04:49,050
the 30 year mortgage rate was

171
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:50,770
about 5.98%.

172
00:04:51,110 --> 00:04:53,010
As oil and inflation jumped,

173
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:56,210
it climbed as high as 7.4%

174
00:04:56,010 --> 00:04:56,590
in October.

175
00:04:57,410 --> 00:04:58,030
This week,

176
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:00,630
it's about 7.24%.

177
00:05:00,959 --> 00:05:02,979
On a $300,000 loan,

178
00:05:03,459 --> 00:05:06,459
that's the difference between about $1,795

179
00:05:06,259 --> 00:05:10,179
and $2,044 a month,

180
00:05:10,579 --> 00:05:14,259
or about $2,996 more every year.

181
00:05:14,693 --> 00:05:16,673
Higher rates also keep many homeowners

182
00:05:16,473 --> 00:05:17,533
from selling,

183
00:05:17,333 --> 00:05:19,193
because they don't want to give

184
00:05:18,993 --> 00:05:20,393
up the low rate they already

185
00:05:20,193 --> 00:05:20,813
have.

186
00:05:21,293 --> 00:05:23,053
That means fewer homes for sale.

187
00:05:23,541 --> 00:05:25,261
Car loans are pricier too.

188
00:05:25,761 --> 00:05:27,161
The average rate on a four

189
00:05:26,961 --> 00:05:28,441
year new car loan from a

190
00:05:28,241 --> 00:05:30,941
bank is about 7.47%.

191
00:05:32,383 --> 00:05:33,783
Higher rates do have a bright

192
00:05:33,583 --> 00:05:34,263
side.

193
00:05:34,683 --> 00:05:35,923
Savers can earn more,

194
00:05:36,023 --> 00:05:37,383
but only if they go looking.

195
00:05:37,882 --> 00:05:39,162
According to the FDIC,

196
00:05:39,302 --> 00:05:41,202
the national average rate on savings

197
00:05:41,002 --> 00:05:44,222
accounts is just 0.38%.

198
00:05:44,789 --> 00:05:47,529
With inflation at 3.7%,

199
00:05:47,329 --> 00:05:50,749
money sitting there loses about 3.3%

200
00:05:50,549 --> 00:05:52,369
of its buying power every year.

201
00:05:52,805 --> 00:05:55,025
Many online banks pay savings rates

202
00:05:54,825 --> 00:05:56,665
much closer to the Fed's rate.

203
00:05:57,185 --> 00:05:58,445
As long as the bank is

204
00:05:58,245 --> 00:05:59,245
FDIC insured,

205
00:05:59,765 --> 00:06:01,605
your deposits are protected up to

206
00:06:01,405 --> 00:06:03,045
$250,000.

207
00:06:03,893 --> 00:06:06,233
On a $10,000 emergency fund,

208
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:10,753
0.38% earns you about $38 a

209
00:06:10,553 --> 00:06:10,973
year.

210
00:06:11,633 --> 00:06:14,673
An account paying 3.5% would earn

211
00:06:14,473 --> 00:06:15,393
about $350.

212
00:06:16,837 --> 00:06:17,937
And if you won't need some

213
00:06:17,737 --> 00:06:18,737
money for a while,

214
00:06:19,037 --> 00:06:20,177
a certificate of deposit,

215
00:06:19,977 --> 00:06:21,137
or CD,

216
00:06:20,937 --> 00:06:23,417
can lock in today's higher rate

217
00:06:23,217 --> 00:06:24,817
for months or even years.

218
00:06:26,083 --> 00:06:27,503
So here's a simple checklist.

219
00:06:28,169 --> 00:06:28,609
One:

220
00:06:28,629 --> 00:06:30,469
pay down variable rate debt first,

221
00:06:30,809 --> 00:06:32,969
starting with whatever charges the highest

222
00:06:32,769 --> 00:06:33,349
rate.

223
00:06:33,838 --> 00:06:34,198
Two:

224
00:06:34,318 --> 00:06:35,658
if you carry a card balance,

225
00:06:35,998 --> 00:06:37,638
call your card company and ask

226
00:06:37,438 --> 00:06:38,518
for a lower rate.

227
00:06:38,978 --> 00:06:40,418
It works more often than you'd

228
00:06:40,218 --> 00:06:40,578
think.

229
00:06:41,043 --> 00:06:41,503
Three:

230
00:06:41,583 --> 00:06:43,143
make sure your savings are actually

231
00:06:42,943 --> 00:06:43,943
earning something.

232
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:44,970
And four:

233
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:46,510
if you're buying a home or

234
00:06:46,310 --> 00:06:46,970
a car,

235
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:48,510
compare at least three lenders.

236
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:50,870
A small difference in rate can

237
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:52,350
add up to thousands of dollars.

238
00:06:52,894 --> 00:06:53,474
And five:

239
00:06:53,534 --> 00:06:54,134
don't panic.

240
00:06:54,594 --> 00:06:55,754
Rates move slowly,

241
00:06:55,554 --> 00:06:56,854
and you have time to make

242
00:06:56,654 --> 00:06:57,174
a plan.

243
00:06:58,450 --> 00:06:58,870
So,

244
00:06:58,670 --> 00:07:00,330
will the Fed raise rates again?

245
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:01,970
It mostly depends on oil.

246
00:07:02,455 --> 00:07:04,615
If shipping through Hormuz recovers and

247
00:07:04,415 --> 00:07:05,695
energy prices fall,

248
00:07:05,955 --> 00:07:07,235
inflation could cool,

249
00:07:07,675 --> 00:07:08,835
and the Fed may not need

250
00:07:08,635 --> 00:07:09,435
to hike again.

251
00:07:09,895 --> 00:07:12,515
If oil stays above $100 and

252
00:07:12,315 --> 00:07:13,935
higher prices keep spreading,

253
00:07:14,195 --> 00:07:15,255
more hikes are possible.

254
00:07:15,885 --> 00:07:17,825
Watch the monthly inflation report.

255
00:07:18,505 --> 00:07:20,085
If energy prices keep easing,

256
00:07:20,405 --> 00:07:21,805
that's a sign the pressure is

257
00:07:21,605 --> 00:07:22,025
fading.

258
00:07:22,599 --> 00:07:24,179
The Fed meets again in December,

259
00:07:24,479 --> 00:07:25,579
and we'll break it down when

260
00:07:25,379 --> 00:07:25,839
it does.

261
00:07:27,083 --> 00:07:28,683
So the Fed raised rates to

262
00:07:28,483 --> 00:07:30,623
fight inflation caused by an oil

263
00:07:30,423 --> 00:07:31,063
shock.

264
00:07:31,563 --> 00:07:32,883
It can't fix the oil,

265
00:07:33,183 --> 00:07:34,563
but it can make borrowing more

266
00:07:34,363 --> 00:07:35,143
expensive,

267
00:07:35,563 --> 00:07:37,703
and that touches almost every part

268
00:07:37,503 --> 00:07:38,823
of your financial life.

269
00:07:39,323 --> 00:07:40,903
Pay down expensive debt,

270
00:07:41,083 --> 00:07:42,623
make your savings work for you,

271
00:07:42,703 --> 00:07:44,103
and compare before you borrow.

272
00:07:44,673 --> 00:07:45,393
Next time:

273
00:07:45,533 --> 00:07:47,133
how banks create money out of

274
00:07:46,933 --> 00:07:47,633
thin air.

275
00:07:48,213 --> 00:07:49,893
Subscribe to Coin Theory so you

276
00:07:49,693 --> 00:07:50,433
don't miss it.

